![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

When validating expected array fields in input data, if a field is present but is not an array, we should record a validation failure (blocker) and process it as an empty array. Additionally, when validating collections of data, we should explicitly check for and record malformed entries as structured validation failures, and skip further processing of those entries to avoid runtime property access errors.

```javascript
export function normalizeSearchGroups(groups, validationErrors = []) {
  if (!isPlainObject(groups)) return {};

  return Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(groups).map(([groupName, results]) => {
      if (results !== undefined && !Array.isArray(results)) {
        validationErrors.push({ type: 'blocker', message: `Expected array for group ${groupName}` });
        return [groupName, []];
      }

      const validResults = [];
      const items = results || [];
      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        const result = items[i];
        if (!isPlainObject(result)) {
          validationErrors.push({ type: 'error', message: `Malformed entry at index ${i} in group ${groupName}` });
          continue;
        }
        validResults.push({
          ...result,
          id: cleanString(result.id ?? result.artifactId ?? result.reviewTaskId, 'result-' + groupName + '-' + i),
          title: cleanString(result.title, 'Untitled result'),
          detail: cleanString(result.detail, ''),
        });
      }
      return [groupName, validResults];
    })
  );
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating expected array fields in input data, if a field is present but is not an array, record a validation failure (blocker) and process it as an empty array. This prevents TypeErrors during execution while ensuring the system fails closed.
2. When validating collections of data, explicitly check for and record malformed entries as structured validation failures, and skip further processing of those entries to avoid runtime property access errors.
</details>

